DependentSet -- option for computing Noetherian operators
*********************************************************

Description
===========

Let $Q \subseteq R := \mathbb{K}[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ be an $d$-dimensional primary
ideal. Then there exists a set of $d$ variables in $R$ which is algebraically
independent in $R/I$. We refer to these as the independent variables, and the
remaining variables are the dependent variables. The function *note
independentSets: (Macaulay2Doc)independentSets, can compute sets of independent
variables for symbolic ideals.

The functions computing Noetherian operators, namely

  * *note noetherianOperators: noetherianOperators, -- Noetherian operators
  * *note specializedNoetherianOperators: specializedNoetherianOperators, --
    Noetherian operators evaluated at a point
  * *note numericalNoetherianOperators: numericalNoetherianOperators, --
    Noetherian operators via numerical interpolation

pass to a polynomial ring in the dependent variables, with the coefficient
field being the fraction field of a polynomial ring in the independent
variables. Because of this, computing Noetherian operators requires a knowledge
of a dependent set of variables, which can be set using the option
DependentSet. Note that the $dx$-monomials will only involve dependent
variables.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x,y];                                                            |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal((x+y)^2);                                                     |
|                                                                             |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : P = radical I;                                                          |
|                                                                             |
|o3 : Ideal of R                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : A = noetherianOperators(I, P, DependentSet => {x})                      |
|                                                                             |
|o4 = {| 1 |, | dx |}                                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o4 : List                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : B = noetherianOperators(I, P, DependentSet => {y})                      |
|                                                                             |
|o5 = {| 1 |, | dy |}                                                         |
|                                                                             |
|o5 : List                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : getIdealFromNoetherianOperators(A, P) ==                                |
|                                                                             |
|o6 = true                                                                    |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|getIdealFromNoetherianOperators(B, P)                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The symbolic method *note noetherianOperators: noetherianOperators, will
usually be able to figure out a dependent set of variables automatically. On
the other hand, numerical computations using *note
specializedNoetherianOperators: specializedNoetherianOperators, and *note
numericalNoetherianOperators: numericalNoetherianOperators, will usually
require the user to set the option DependentSet.



Caveat
======

The option DependentSet is ignored when calling *note noetherianOperators:
noetherianOperators, with *note Strategy => "PunctualQuot": Strategy =>
"PunctualQuot",. Note that this is the default strategy for *note
noetherianOperators(Ideal): noetherianOperators_lpIdeal_rp,.



