eliminatingDual -- eliminating dual space of a polynomial ideal
***************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        S = eliminatingDual(p, I, v, d)
  * Inputs:
      * p, a *note point: (NAGtypes)AbstractPoint,, 
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, or a one-row *note matrix:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix, of generators
      * v, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, a list of the integers
        designating which variables to bound
      * d, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, the degree bound for the
        designated variables
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Tolerance: Tolerance _lpNoetherianOperators_rp, => ..., default
        value null, optional argument for numerical tolerance
  * Outputs:
      * S, an instance of the type *note DualSpace: (NAGtypes)DualSpace,, 

Description
===========

Given a list of variable indices, this method computes a basis for all dual
elements orthogonal to I which have total degree in the variables on the list
bounded by d.

+---------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = CC[x,y];                            |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal{x^2-y^3}                      |
|                                             |
|              3    2                         |
|o2 = ideal(- y  + x )                        |
|                                             |
|o2 : Ideal of R                              |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i3 : eliminatingDual(origin R, I, {0}, 2)    |
|                                             |
|o3 = | y5+x2y2 y4+x2y y3+x2 xy2 xy x y2 y 1 ||
|                                             |
|o3 : DualSpace                               |
+---------------------------------------------+

This function generalizes *note truncatedDual: truncatedDual, in that if v
includes all the variables in the ring, then its behavior is the same.

+-------------------------------------------+
|i4 : eliminatingDual(origin R, I, {0,1}, 2)|
|                                           |
|o4 = | xy y2 x y 1 |                       |
|                                           |
|o4 : DualSpace                             |
+-------------------------------------------+

See also *note truncatedDual: truncatedDual,.

Caveat
======

The space of dual elements satisfying the conditions is not in general of
finite dimension. If the dimension is infinite, this function will not
terminate.  This is not checked.  To ensure termination, the local dimension of
I at p should not exceed the length of v, and certain genericity constraints on
the coordinates must be met.

Ways to use eliminatingDual:
============================

  * "eliminatingDual(AbstractPoint,Ideal,List,ZZ)"
  * "eliminatingDual(AbstractPoint,Matrix,List,ZZ)"

