getIdealFromNoetherianOperators -- Computes a primary ideal corresponding to a list of Noetherian operators and a prime ideal
*****************************************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        Q = getIdealFromNoetherianOperators(L, P)
  * Inputs:
      * L, a *note list: (Macaulay2Doc)List,, of *note differential operators:
        DiffOp,
      * P, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, a prime ideal
  * Outputs:
      * Q, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, the primary ideal
        corresponding to L and P

Description
===========

This method contains an implementation of Algorithm 3.9 in the paper Primary
ideals and their differential equations (see https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.04700
). This method can be seen as the reverse operation of computing a set of
Noetherian operators for a primary ideal.



Let $R$ be a polynomial ring $R = K[x_1,\ldots,x_n]$ over a field $K$ of
characteristic zero. Consider the Weyl algebra $D = R<dx_1,\ldots,dx_n>$ and a
list of differential operators $L = \{L_1,\ldots,L_m\} \,\subset\, D$. Denote
by $\mathcal{E} \,\subset\, D$ the $R$-bisubmodule of $D$ that is generated by
$L_1,\ldots,L_m$. For a given prime ideal $P \,\subset\, R$, this method
computes the $P$-primary ideal given as $$ Q = \{ f \,\in\, R\, \mid\, \delta\,
\bullet\, f\, \in P, \ \forall  \delta \in \mathcal{E} \}. $$

Next, we provide several examples to show the interplay between computing a set
of Noetherian operators and then recovering the original ideal.

The first example shows an ideal that can be described with two different sets
of Noetherian operators (this example appeared in Example 7.8 of the paper
Primary ideals and their differential equations (see
https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.04700 )).

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4]                                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o1 = R                                                                       |
|                                                                             |
|o1 : PolynomialRing                                                          |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : MM = matrix {{x_3,x_1,x_2},{x_1,x_2,x_4}}                               |
|                                                                             |
|o2 = | x_3 x_1 x_2 |                                                         |
|     | x_1 x_2 x_4 |                                                         |
|                                                                             |
|             2      3                                                        |
|o2 : Matrix R  <-- R                                                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : P = minors(2,MM)                                                        |
