zeroDimensionalDual -- dual space of a zero-dimensional polynomial ideal
************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        S = zeroDimensionalDual(p, I)
  * Inputs:
      * p, a *note point: (NAGtypes)AbstractPoint,, or a one-row *note matrix:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, or a one-row *note matrix:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix, of generators
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Tolerance: Tolerance _lpNoetherianOperators_rp, => ..., default
        value null, optional argument for numerical tolerance
  * Outputs:
      * S, an instance of the type *note DualSpace: (NAGtypes)DualSpace,, 

Description
===========

This function computes a reduced basis of the dual space of a zero-dimensional
ideal.  It does not check if the ideal is zero-dimensional and if not then
termination will fail. Elements are expressed as elements of the polynomial
ring of the ideal although this is an abuse of notation. They are really
elements of the dual ring.

+---------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = QQ[a,b];                                  |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal{a^3,b^3}                            |
|                                                   |
|             3   3                                 |
|o2 = ideal (a , b )                                |
|                                                   |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                    |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : D = zeroDimensionalDual(origin(R), I)         |
|                                                   |
|o3 = | 1 b a 1/2b2 ab 1/2a2 1/2ab2 1/2a2b 1/4a2b2 ||
|                                                   |
|o3 : DualSpace                                     |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : dim D                                         |
|                                                   |
|o4 = 9                                             |
+---------------------------------------------------+

The dimension of the dual space at p is the multiplicity of the solution at p.

+-------------------------------------------+
|i5 : S = CC[x,y];                          |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i6 : J = ideal{(y-2)^2,y-x^2}              |
|                                           |
|             2              2              |
|o6 = ideal (y  - 4y + 4, - x  + y)         |
|                                           |
|o6 : Ideal of S                            |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i7 : p = point matrix{{1.4142136_CC,2_CC}};|
+-------------------------------------------+
|i8 : D = zeroDimensionalDual(p, J)         |
|                                           |
|o8 = | 1 .353553x+y |                      |
|                                           |
|o8 : DualSpace                             |
+-------------------------------------------+
|i9 : dim D                                 |
|                                           |
|o9 = 2                                     |
+-------------------------------------------+

Caveat
======

The computation will not terminate if I is not locally zero-dimensional at the
chosen point.  This is not checked.

See also
========

  * *note truncatedDual: truncatedDual, -- truncated dual space of a polynomial
    ideal

Ways to use zeroDimensionalDual:
================================

  * "zeroDimensionalDual(AbstractPoint,Ideal)"
  * "zeroDimensionalDual(AbstractPoint,Matrix)"
  * "zeroDimensionalDual(Matrix,Ideal)"
  * "zeroDimensionalDual(Matrix,Matrix)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note zeroDimensionalDual: zeroDimensionalDual, is a *note method
function with options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

